   MAY 11-15, 2016},
Organization = {Assoc Comp Machinery; Int WWW Conf Comm; ACM SIGWEB; Google; Microsoft;
   Palais Congres Montreal; Tourisme Montreal; Yahoo Res; Baidu; Bookwitty;
   Cisco; YelloPages; IBM Res; UQAM},
Abstract = {In this position paper we present the challenge of detecting abuse in a
   modern Online Social Network (OSN) while balancing data utility and
   privacy, with the goal of limiting the amount of user sensitive
   information processed during data collection, extraction and analysis.
   While we are working with public domain data available in a contemporary
   OSN, our goal is to design a thorough method for future alternative OSN
   designs that both protect user's sensitive information and discourage
   abuse.
   In this summary, we present initial results for detecting abusive
   behavior on Twitter. We plan to further investigate the impact of
   reducing input metadata on the quality of the abuse detection. In
   addition, we will consider defeating Byzantine behavior by opponents in
   the system.},
