   Association-for-Computational-Linguistics (ACL), Dublin, IRELAND, MAY
   22-27, 2022},
Organization = {Assoc Computat Linguist; OE Gaillimh NUI Galway; Insight; Sci Fdn
   Ireland; Digital Univ; IIITM Kerala; Univ Hyderabad; Univ Jaffna; Tamil
   Virtual Acad},
Abstract = {In social media, there are instances where people present their opinions
   in strong language, resorting to abusive/toxic comments.There are
   instances of communal hatred, hate-speech, toxicity and bullying. And,
   in this age of social media, it's very important to find means to keep
   check on these toxic comments, as to preserve the mental peace of people
   in social media. While there are tools, models to detect and potentially
   filter these kind of content, developing these kinds of models for the
   low resource language space is an issue of research.
   In this paper, the task of abusive comment identification in Tamil
   language, is seen upon as a multiclass classification problem.There are
   different pre-processing as well as modelling approaches discussed in
   this paper.The different approaches are compared on the basis of
   weighted average accuracy.},
